WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.003
A practical secret storage roadmap begins with identification and classification.

2
00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:14.412
Decide what is a secret, what it grants, who owns it, which environment it belongs to, how long it should live, and which component truly needs

3
00:00:14.412 --> 00:00:14.813
access.

4
00:00:14.813 --> 00:00:20.017
Avoid hardcoded and embedded values in source, binaries, documentation, images, and test fixtures.

5
00:00:20.017 --> 00:00:28.424
If a secret is committed or packaged, treat removal and rotation as part of the response, not as separate optional cleanup.

6
00:00:28.424 --> 00:00:30.827
Use approved storage and delivery mechanisms.

7
00:00:30.827 --> 00:00:37.632
Prefer platform secret stores, managed vaults, orchestrator features, hardware-backed storage, or vetted libraries over custom schemes.

8
00:00:37.632 --> 00:00:43.638
Document how secrets reach the application, and reject unsafe delivery paths in production where possible.

9
00:00:43.638 --> 00:00:46.040
Keep runtime exposure short and intentional.

10
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:54.847
Minimize copies, avoid broad ownership, do not log secret values, and use secure-memory facilities when the assurance requirement calls for them.

11
00:00:54.847 --> 00:01:00.452
For file-based secrets, control the path, permissions, key management, backups, and cleanup behavior.

12
00:01:00.452 --> 00:01:04.856
Apply least privilege so applications receive only the secrets they need.

13
00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:07.258
Make rotation and revocation tested routines.

14
00:01:07.258 --> 00:01:10.060
Keep audit records useful without revealing values.

15
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:15.265
Review logs, tests, crash reports, support bundles, build artifacts, dependencies, and deployment settings.

16
00:01:15.265 --> 00:01:24.072
When a new service, library, or pipeline step is added, ask how it receives secrets and where those values can be preserved.

17
00:01:24.072 --> 00:01:26.875
Secure secret storage is a lifecycle practice.

18
00:01:26.875 --> 00:01:32.880
It succeeds when teams make safe handling visible, repeatable, and governed across development and operations.
